KG42.register("rules-391c2975a79c530ab2ca66bb",{"id":"07-nash_bargaining::v141-c-R007","paper":"07-nash_bargaining","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R007","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Prosumer participation","process_stage":"participation","market_meaning":"Microgrids with excess renewable generation may participate in energy trading with microgrids in deficit of power supplies for mutual benefit. Scope: Energy trading among interconnected autonomous microgrids in a distribution network. Condition: The supplying microgrid has excess renewable generation and the prospective receiving microgrid has a power-supply deficit.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function participation.prosumer under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["participation.prosumer"],"E":[],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Energy trading among interconnected autonomous microgrids in a distribution network.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01"]},"x":[],"theta":[],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R007-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Microgrids with excess renewable generation may participate in energy trading with microgrids in deficit of power supplies for mutual benefit. Scope: Energy trading among interconnected autonomous microgrids in a distribution network. Condition: The supplying microgrid has excess renewable generation and the prospective receiving microgrid has a power-supply deficit.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.prosumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e01"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R007-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"supporting","statement":"A microgrid will only participate in the energy trading if it can reduce its overall cost; otherwise it may choose not to participate. The set M' ⊆ M denotes microgrids willing to trade energy with each other. Microgrids outside M' cannot benefit from energy trading and have no incentive to participate. Participation is voluntary and conditional on cost improvement relative to the no-trade benchmark.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.prosumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-e05","v141-b-g01-e06","v141-b-g02-E13","v141-b-g02-E20","v141-b-g02-E21"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p01-c1r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":1,"printed_page":null,"section":"Abstract","anchor_text":"Abstract In this paper, we study the interactions among di interconnected autonomous microgrids, and","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-01.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In this paper, we study the interactions among interconnected autonomous microgrids, and develop a joint energy trading and scheduling strategy. Each interconnected microgrid not only schedules its local power supply and demand, but also trades energy with other microgrids in a distribution network. Specifically, microgrids with excessive renewable generations can trade with other microgrids in deficit of power supplies for mutual benefits. Since interconnected microgrids operate autonomously, they aim to optimize their own performance and expect to gain benefits through energy trading."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e05","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"an Ce(πi) = πi,j. me j∈M\\i he The overall cost of microgrid i consists of both operat- n- ing cost C","equation_id":"(18)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"It is clear that a microgrid will only participate in the trading if it can reduce its overall cost. This means that some microgrid may choose not participate in the trading. Thus, we have the following constraint: CO_i(q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i}) + C_e(π_i) ≤ C_i^{Non}, ∀i ∈ M. (18) Here the left-hand side of the inequality counts the overall cost of microgrid i when participating in the energy trading. In the right-hand side, C_i^{Non} denotes the minimized cost that microgrid i can achieve without trading energy with other microgrids. In the bargaining literature we also call C_i^{Non} the disagreement point."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e06","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M01-participation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"III.D Nash Bargaining Formulation for Energy Trading","anchor_text":"all unt D. Nash Bargaining Formulation for Energy Trading o- We let M′ ⊆M denote the set of microgri","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"We let M′ ⊆ M denote the set of microgrids who are willing to trade energy with each other. For the microgrids j ∈ M\\M′, they cannot benefit from energy trading and thus have no incentive to participate in the energy trading. Therefore, we focus on the microgrids in set M′, and formulate their energy trading as a Nash bargaining problem:"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E13","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":5,"printed_page":"2651","section":"III.C Energy Trading Among Microgrids","anchor_text":"an Ce(πi) = πi,j. me j∈M\\i he The overall cost of microgrid i consists of both operat- n- ing cost C","equation_id":"(18)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"It is clear that a microgrid will only participate in the trading if it can reduce its overall cost. This means that some microgrid may choose not participate in the trading. Thus, we have the following constraint:\nCO_i(q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i}) + Ce(π_i) ≤ C_i^{Non},   ∀i ∈ M.   (18)\nHere the left-hand side of the inequality counts the overall cost of microgrid i when participating in the energy trading. In the right-hand side, C_i^{Non} denotes the minimized cost that microgrid i can achieve without trading energy with other microgrids. In the bargaining literature we also call C_i^{Non} the disagreement point."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E20","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p06-c2r1__v141-a-M01-participation","page":6,"printed_page":"2652","section":"IV. PROBLEM ANALYSIS","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SMART GRID, VOL. 9, NO. 4, JULY 2018 he thus each such microgrid i achieves the","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"thus each such microgrid i achieves the same performance of C_i^{Non} as in Section III-B, when they operate separately from other microgrids. Therefore, only those microgrids in set M′ that trade energy with others contribute to the total cost reduction of the system."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-7e85444402-p07-c1r1__v141-a-M01-participation","page":7,"printed_page":"2653","section":"IV. PROBLEM ANALYSIS","anchor_text":"WANG AND HUANG: INCENTIVIZING ENERGY TRADING FOR INTERCONNECTED M microgrids do not contribute to th","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"microgrids do not contribute to the social cost minimization and won't benefit from energy trading. The only criterion for determining trading and non-trading microgrids is whether they can contribute to the social cost minimization. Secondly, after identifying those microgrids who do not want to participate in the energy trading, we then focus on the rest of the microgrids who trade energy. We decide a fair allocation of benefits to each of participated microgrids through the payment bargaining problem P2."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":"C explicitly supplied only source-backed unary facts for this function; other graph gaps remain reviewable.","empty_x_reason":"No decision variable is defined within this rule's scope. The source expresses a permission/eligibility condition ('microgrids with excessive renewable generations can trade...'), not an optimization variable. Scheduling/trading variables such as g_i, q_i, x_n, r_{c,i}, r_{d,i}, e_i and payment variables such as π_i appear in other rules' scopes and cannot be imported into R007 without changing the frozen rule identity or scope.","empty_theta_reason":"No fixed input, bound, coefficient, or exogenous constant is declared for this participation rule. The verified evidence for this rule gives no numeric parameter or symbolic constant. Candidate parameters such as G_i, β_n, C_i^{Non}, or π_i belong to other rule scopes and are not defined here."},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-participation"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"participation.prosumer","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-e01","quote":"Specifically, microgrids with excessive renewable generations can trade with other microgrids in deficit of power supplies for mutual benefits."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"2b4c58251164c987476438b092b3709078f4a377d07f585cd6188948ce566ba3","applicability":"non_mathematical","reason":"The frozen R007 is a participation/admission rule: the source states who may trade (microgrids with excessive renewable generation and counterparties with a power-supply deficit) and why (mutual benefit). This is a prose eligibility condition, not an equation or optimization problem; the rule's single frozen node participation.prosumer is a unary participation predicate and the frozen edge set is empty. The verified evidence supplied for this rule (v141-b-g01-e01) contains no symbol definition, coefficient, bound, index, or constraint expression to transcribe. Mathematical participation conditions elsewhere (e.g., the individual-rationality inequality (18)) require variables, parameters and graph relations that are outside R007's frozen V/E and are assigned to other rule scopes; importing them would create cross-rule scope mixing and would not be a faithful formalization of this frozen rule.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R007-C001","node_ids":["participation.prosumer"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C001 is the frozen core procedural constraint of R007. It is attached to the only node in the rule's frozen V, participation.prosumer. The frozen edge set E is empty, so no edge binding is possible. This binding records the unary participation predicate without asserting any additional graph relation."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R007-C002","node_ids":["participation.prosumer"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C002 is a supporting participation/eligibility constraint built only from evidence already in this rule's same-mechanism scope. It concerns the same participation.prosumer node and is bound to it. R007's frozen graph has no edges, so edge_ids is empty."}],"revision_requests":[]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
